  "answer": "Based on NCES data from Fall 2021 (Digest of Education Statistics 2022, Table 203.40 for public enrollment; Digest of Education Statistics 2023, Table 205.80 for private enrollment), among states with fewer than 1 million public school students, the 5 with the lowest percentage of private school students relative to total (public + private) enrollment are:\n\n| Rank | State | Public Enrollment | Private Enrollment | Total Enrollment | Private % |\n|------|-------|-------------------|-------------------|------------------|-----------|\n| 1 | **Wyoming** | 93,093 | 2,270 | 95,363 | **2.38%** |\n| 2 | **Utah** | 690,934 | 25,870 | 716,804 | **3.61%** |\n| 3 | **Alaska** | 129,944 | 5,080 | 135,024 | **3.76%** |\n| 4 | **West Virginia** | 252,720 | 13,660 | 266,380 | **5.13%** |\n| 5 | **Arkansas** | 489,565 | 28,230 | 517,795 | **5.45%** |\n\n**Note:** Oklahoma (5.50%) and Nevada (5.59%) rank just behind Arkansas. All calculations use Fall 2021 data from the Common Core of Data (public) and Private School Universe Survey (private).",
